---
source: crates/aozora-cli/tests/snapshot_cli.rs
expression: "run(&[\"repl\", \"--help\"])"
---
Start an interactive read-eval-print loop: type a line of notation and see its parsed nodes,
rendered HTML, Pandoc AST, and diagnostics immediately (the terminal counterpart to the web
playground). Reuses the same parse / render / json engine as the document subcommands, so its views
can never disagree with them. `:mode` / `:lang` / `:encoding` / `:load` / `:help` / `:quit` tune the
session; line editing and history come from rustyline on a terminal, and a piped stdin is scriptable

Usage: aozora repl [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --mode <MODE>
          Which view(s) to show for each line: `nodes`, `html`, `pandoc`, or `all` (the default).
          Changeable in-session with `:mode`

          Possible values:
          - nodes:  The parsed source nodes as JSON (as `aozora inspect nodes`)
          - html:   The rendered HTML (as `aozora render`)
          - pandoc: The Pandoc AST as JSON (as `aozora pandoc`)
          - all:    Every view at once: nodes, HTML, and Pandoc
          
          [default: all]

  -E, --encoding <ENCODING>
          Source encoding for `:load`: `auto` (default), `utf8`, or `sjis`. Typed lines are already
          UTF-8, so this only governs file loads. Changeable in-session with `:encoding`

          Possible values:
          - auto: Detect the source encoding: valid UTF-8 is used as-is, otherwise the bytes are
            decoded as Shift_JIS. The right default — Aozora files ship as Shift_JIS, but UTF-8
            mirrors are common, and the caller should not have to know which they have
          - utf8: Force UTF-8; error if the input is not valid UTF-8
          - sjis: Force Shift_JIS decoding
          
          [default: auto]

      --color <WHEN>
          When to colourise diagnostics: `auto` (colour on a terminal, honouring `NO_COLOR` /
          `CLICOLOR` / `CLICOLOR_FORCE`), `always`, or `never`. Global — accepted after any
          subcommand. Governs `check`'s graphical diagnostics; `spec kinds` tables are always
          monochrome

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour when stdout is a terminal (honours `NO_COLOR` / `CLICOLOR` /
            `CLICOLOR_FORCE`)
          - always: Always colour, even when piped
          - never:  Never colour
          
          [default: auto]

  -v, --verbose...
          Increase log verbosity (repeatable): `-v` info, `-vv` debug, `-vvv` trace. Logs go to
          stderr only, so stdout — and the JSON / short diagnostic streams — stay byte-identical.
          `AOZORA_LOG` overrides this; cancels against `--quiet` (one axis). Global

  -q, --quiet
          Lower log verbosity to errors only — the opposite end of the `--verbose` axis (a single
          `-v` cancels it). Affects only stderr logging, never stdout. `AOZORA_LOG` overrides it.
          Global

      --lang <LOCALE>
          Language for human messages (the stdin guard, the `--watch` banner, and `explain`), as a
          BCP-47 tag: `en` (the default), `ja`, or `zh`. Highest priority in `--lang > AOZORA_LANG >
          .aozora.toml lang > LANG`; unknown locales fall back to `en`. Never affects machine output
          (json / short / codes / exit / schema) or `--encoding`. Global

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Examples:
  aozora repl                    # start the loop (all views)
  aozora repl --mode html        # start showing only rendered HTML
  aozora repl -E sjis            # decode :load files as Shift_JIS

Inside the loop, type notation to see it parsed, or a :command:
  :mode html   :lang ja   :encoding sjis   :load FILE   :help   :quit
